We find that {{<String>}} component always do a Suspense suspend/resume, one time, on first render, even when the string we want is already there in {{M.str}} cache.

To produce the bug is to add this test to {{public/lib/js/esm/tests/String.test.tsx}}:

{noformat}it('shows the fallback for an instant before an already-cached string, even with no real fetch', async() => {
        // Cache the string BEFORE rendering, simulating one Moodle already loaded on the page.
        (globalThis as any).mockString('all', 'core', 'All');

        const {container} = render(<String identifier="all" component="core">{'loading'}</String>);

        // Check the DOM right away, before waiting for anything async.
        expect(container.textContent).toBe('loading');
    });{noformat}

The result is passed, but it shouldn't be because the expected result is {{All}}, not {{loading}}

h2. Summary

The blocks drawer skip links (for example, "Skip Table of contents") do not become visible when they receive keyboard focus, even though they are focusable and can be activated. This affects all browsers on main and is a [WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible (Level AA)|https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-visible.html] issue.

h2. Problem

Every skip link uses the {{visually-hidden-focusable}} pattern: hidden until focused, then revealed. The site-level "Skip to main content" link reveals correctly on focus. The skip links generated for blocks in the blocks drawer (rendered by {{core/block}}, for example the mod_book "Table of contents" block) do not reveal: the link stays visually hidden even while focused. It can still be activated with Enter and focus moves to the target, so it is a visibility problem only.

h2. Steps to reproduce

# Open an activity that puts a block in the blocks drawer, for example a Book with a couple of chapters (the "Table of contents" block).
# Make sure the blocks drawer is open.
# Using the keyboard, Tab to the "Skip Table of contents" link.

*Expected*: the skip link becomes visible while focused, the same way "Skip to main content" does.

*Actual*: the skip link stays invisible. Nothing appears on screen even though the link is focused (and pressing Enter works).

Reproduces in Chrome, Firefox and Safari on main. Does not reproduce on stable branches.

h2. Affected context

* Branch: main only. It is a regression from MDL-88411; stable branches do not have the anchored-drawer {{clip-path}} and are not affected.
* Browsers: all.
* Layouts: pages where the blocks drawer is anchored, that is {{pagelayout-standard}} and {{body.limitedwidth}}, at the {{lg}} breakpoint and up. mod_book view pages use {{limitedwidth}}, so they are affected.

h2. Root cause

Two CSS rules interact badly:

# The reveal rule pins any focused skip link to the top-left corner of the viewport:
{noformat}// theme/boost/scss/moodle/core.scss
.visually-hidden-focusable:focus,
.visually-hidden-focusable:active {
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    z-index: 1031;
}{noformat}
# MDL-88411 gave the anchored blocks drawer a {{clip-path}}, used to animate opening and to keep its content within its box:
{noformat}// theme/boost/scss/moodle/drawer.scss (inside .pagelayout-standard, body.limitedwidth)
.drawer.drawer-right.show {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
}{noformat}

{{clip-path}} clips the painting of the whole subtree, including descendants that have escaped layout containment via {{position: fixed}}. So when a drawer skip link is focused and jumps to {{position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0}} (the viewport corner, outside the drawer's box), the drawer's {{clip-path}} clips it away and it is never painted. This is not browser-specific: it was confirmed by forcing the link to {{position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0}} with a bright background, after which the link is present in the DOM with real dimensions but nothing paints at the corner.

"Skip to main content" is never affected because it lives at the top of {{<body>}}, not inside the clipped drawer.

Related existing code: {{drawer.scss}} already carries a workaround for a different symptom of the same {{position: fixed}} reveal (raising the drawer {{z-index}} on {{:focus-within}} so the fixed link is not hidden behind the navbar). That workaround does not address the clip-path clipping.

As part of migrating the Timeline block to React, the PHP code currently mounts the React component by manually generating the required HTML and serialising the component props.

For example:

{code:javascript}$props = json_encode($props, JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE | JSON_HEX_TAG);

$mount = html_writer::div('', '', [
    'data-react-component' => '@moodle/lms/block_timeline/Timeline',
    'data-react-props' => $props,
]);{code}

This exposes the implementation details of the React mounting mechanism to feature code. Consumers are required to understand:

*  the required {{data-*}} attributes; 
*  how props should be serialised; 
*  which JSON encoding options should be used; 
*  how the mount element should be constructed. 

This should instead be provided through a supported PHP API so that feature code only needs to specify the React component and its props. The implementation details of the mounting mechanism should remain internal to the frontend platform.

Providing a standard API would:

*  reduce boilerplate when creating React entry points; 
*  ensure consistent prop serialisation and escaping; 
*  hide the DOM contract used by the React autoinit system; 
*  allow the mounting implementation to evolve without requiring changes throughout core and plugins. 

The Timeline block can continue using the current approach until such an API is available, but future React components should be encouraged to use the new abstraction.

A new interface should also be considered to perform in a similar way as {{named_templatable}} to provide a simplified migration path from templates to React. This should allow the react component entrypoint to be generated based upon a renderable being rendered, for example:

{code:php}$renderable = new \block_timeline\output\main($sort, $filter, $limit);
$renderer = $this->page->get_renderer('block_timeline');
$this->content = (object) [
    'text' => $renderer->render($renderable),
    'footer' => '',
];{code}

The react interface should take precedence over the {{named_templatable}} interface, and have lower precedent than a specific {{render_[renderable]}} method.

*Acceptance criteria*

* A supported PHP API exists for mounting React components. 
* Feature code specifies only the component identifier and props. 
* Prop serialisation and escaping are handled internally.
* The DOM contract used by the React autoinit system is hidden from consumers. 
* Existing consumers can be migrated to the new API.
* A renderable react component interface exists.
** Which automatically handles rendering using the PHP API when a renderable is rendered using the {{render}} method
** Which has a higher precedent than {{named_templatable}}
** Which has a lower precedent than a correclty named {{render_[renderable_name]}} method in a renderer class.

The goal of this issue is not to change how React components are mounted, but to provide a stable abstraction over the current implementation so that feature and plugin developers are not coupled to its internal details.

I’ve noticed that whenever we use a relative import in our ESM code it’s kept relative in the built file, for example:



{noformat}import {relativeUrl} from '@moodle/lms/core/url';
import {getGlobalAbortSignal} from './abort';{noformat}

Becomes:

{code:javascript}import { relativeUrl } from "@moodle/lms/core/url";
import { getGlobalAbortSignal } from "./abort.js";{code}

Unfortunately relative imports are not properly supported when it comes to Swizzling because an import from somewhere like a theme overridden component ({{@moodle/lms/theme-boost/core/ajax}}) will become {{@moodle/lms/theme-boost/core/abort}} rather than the {{theme-original}} variant.
